      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(i^{11} + i^{111}\\), we need to understand the properties of the imaginary unit \\(i\\). The imaginary unit \\(i\\) is defined as \\(i = \\sqrt{-1}\\), and it has a cyclical pattern in its powers:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= i^2 \\cdot i = -1 \\cdot i = -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= i^3 \\cdot i = (-i) \\cdot i = -i^2 = -(-1) = 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i^4 \\cdot i = 1 \\cdot i = i, \\\\\n&\\vdots\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThis pattern repeats every 4 powers. Therefore, to find \\(i^{11}\\) and \\(i^{111}\\), we can reduce the exponents modulo 4.\n\nFirst, let's find \\(i^{11}\\):\n\n\\[\n11 \\div 4 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad i^{11} = i^{4 \\cdot 2 + 3} = (i^4)^2 \\cdot i^3 = 1^2 \\cdot (-i) = -i\n\\]\n\nNext, let's find \\(i^{111}\\):\n\n\\[\n111 \\div 4 = 27 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad i^{111} = i^{4 \\cdot 27 + 3} = (i^4)^{27} \\cdot i^3 = 1^{27} \\cdot (-i) = -i\n\\]\n\nNow, we can add the results:\n\n\\[\ni^{11} + i^{111} = -i + (-i) = -2i\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2i}\n\\]",
