    "text": "To evaluate \\(i^{11} + i^{111}\\), we need to understand the properties of the imaginary unit \\(i\\). The imaginary unit \\(i\\) is defined as \\(i = \\sqrt{-1}\\), and it has a cyclic pattern in its powers:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= i^2 \\cdot i = -1 \\cdot i = -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= i^3 \\cdot i = -i \\cdot i = -i^2 = -(-1) = 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i^4 \\cdot i = 1 \\cdot i = i, \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand so on. This pattern repeats every 4 powers. Therefore, to find \\(i^n\\), we can reduce the exponent \\(n\\) modulo 4:\n\n\\[\ni^n = i^{n \\mod 4}.\n\\]\n\nLet's apply this to \\(i^{11}\\) and \\(i^{111}\\):\n\n1. For \\(i^{11}\\):\n\\[\n11 \\mod 4 = 3 \\implies i^{11} = i^3 = -i.\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(i^{111}\\):\n\\[\n111 \\mod 4 = 3 \\implies i^{111} = i^3 = -i.\n\\]\n\nNow, we can add the results:\n\\[\ni^{11} + i^{111} = -i + (-i) = -2i.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2i}.\n\\]",
